<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Momeik State</h1><p class="st-meta">1238 CE – 1959 CE (approx.) · capital Momeik</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Shan state in Myanmar's Shan hills, near the ruby mines of Mogok, that kept its hereditary rulers until 1959.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Momeik, Shan Mong Mit, traces its ruling house to the 13th century in Shan tradition, and for much of its early history it was closely linked to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi</a>. It lay north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a> valley and the Shan plateau.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its saopha ruled valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> worked by Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaung">Palaung</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-people">Kachin peoples</a>, under the overlordship first of Ava and later of the Toungoo and Konbaung kings. The state's territory at times included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogok">Mogok</a>, whose rubies made the region famous, though the mines were usually under direct royal control.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the British annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> in 1885, Momeik became one of the Shan states under British protection and later part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a>. Like other saophas, its ruler surrendered his powers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> government in 1959.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1238 CE – 1959 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.34%;width:14.35%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1238 CE – 1959 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Momeik</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Momeik, Mong Mit</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1598&amp;focus=momeik-state" aria-label="Momeik State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7262 1325 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Momeik State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7582 1581L7576 1622M7582 1581L7616 1809M7582 1581L7611 1665"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7576 1622h0M7616 1809h0M7611 1665h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7582 1581h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7582 1581h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Momeik. <a href="/atlas?year=1598&amp;focus=momeik-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

